WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Next we are going to talk about infant CPR which applies to any baby up to one

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.440
year old. Much like with older children, cardiac arrest in infants usually starts

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.880
as a respiratory issue rather than a heart problem. While the situation is

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.020
different, you will find that the core steps of the rescue remain the same ones

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:25.080
you have already learned. One, ensure the scene is safe before you approach. Two,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.120
assess the infant for responsiveness. Because a baby can't tell you if they

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.200
are okay, you will check for a reaction by tickling the infant's foot or

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.200
pinching their toe. While you do this, check for breathing. Is their chest

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.240
rising and falling? Look for those severe signs we discussed earlier. A lack of

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.919
consciousness or blue lips and extremities. If the baby moves when you

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:54.840
tickle their foot, that is a great sign, but you should still call 911 if you

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:01.439
feel something is wrong. Three, call for help and activate 911. If the infant is

00:01:01.439 --> 00:01:07.239
unresponsive and not breathing, call 911 immediately and place them on speaker

00:01:07.239 --> 00:01:11.919
phones so you can begin CPR while talking to the dispatcher. There is one

00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:15.839
major difference for infants if you are alone and do not have a phone. You should

00:01:15.839 --> 00:01:21.040
perform five rounds of CPR first. If the baby is still unresponsive after those

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.720
rounds, pick the baby up and take them with you to find a phone or help. You

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.919
never want to leave an infant alone while you go to get assistance.

